WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.380
Washington, DC, gets blown up all the time —

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.880
in the movies.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.260
But what if it happened for real?

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.260
Hopefully, we’ll never find out.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.620
But federal emergency officials

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.940
have to prepare for the worst.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.260
Emergency responders would have to deal with

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.100
shattered buildings, rubble-filled streets,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.960
downed power lines, fried cell phones,

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.480
radioactive contamination, and casualties numbering in

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.630
the hundreds of thousands.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.120
It would be a disaster beyond anything

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.540
ever seen in an American city -- certainly beyond

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.420
anything that emergency managers

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.940
have ever experienced.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.960
So, to help them figure out what to expect

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.380
in such a catastrophe, researchers from Virginia

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.960
Tech university have built an entire digital DC

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.180
inside a computer — and have blown it up

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.540
over and over and over.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.100
In an effort to make their model

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.100
as accurate as possible,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.660
they start with the basic geography

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.299
of Washington.

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:06.870
Then they add in every building …

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.550
Every road…

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.150
Every power line…

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:11.660
Every hospital…

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.120
Even every cell tower.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.360
Next, the modelers populate their virtual DC

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.700
with millions of digital people, known as ‘agents’.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.600
These agents are like members of the crowd

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.420
in a computer-generated scene in a movie:

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.980
They can move around the landscape

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.460
and react to each other and their surroundings

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.020
in human-like ways.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.080
But unlike CGI crowds,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.720
each agent pursues its own goals.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.980
they can switch between goals like ‘run’, ‘hide’,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.840
‘help out’, and ‘find loved ones.’

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.440
Finally, the modelers simulate the effects

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.540
of a 10-kiloton nuclear bomb that explodes

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.320
on a Monday morning in May,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.260
just a few blocks north of the White House.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.780
They use standard models of blast effects

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.900
to estimate casualties and damage to buildings.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.320
They use weather models to track the spread of fallout.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.460
But they allow the agents

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.540
to react to the disaster as individuals.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.700
This gives the agents’ bottom-up behavior

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.160
the kind of spontaneity that’s so often

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.700
seen in real life — and that is almost impossible

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.840
to model in any other way.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.820
It also gives emergency managers

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.820
a much better handle on the

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.330
surprising and obstinate ways

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.160
that people often behave in a crisis.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.820
For example, if the authorities warn everyone

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.480
to find shelter and stay there until the fallout

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.400
dies down, the model shows

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.540
that many people will ignore them.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.760
Instead, they will be out on the roads,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.040
desperately trying to pick up their kids from school,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.820
or find missing spouses.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.420
Many of them may even end up

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.340
traveling toward ground zero.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.420
But the model also shows that the authorities

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.900
can minimize this chaos if they move quickly

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.080
to restore cell service: if people can verify

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.300
that their loved ones are safe, they are more

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.800
likely to stay where they are.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.360
This disaster-response simulation is just

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.100
one example of what ‘agent-based’ models can do.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.320
More and more real-world decision-makers

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.160
are using these models to plan new highways,

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.940
improve the flow of crowds in public places,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.220
plan how to respond to epidemics —

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.320
or even minimize the chances of a stock market crash.

